# AMOUNTS {.unnumbered}



These graphs display counts or simple summary statistics of one or two columns in a dataset. Each chapter draws the data we expect first. Then we change the data to show what a quick look misses, like an average that hides two clumps or one odd cell in a grid.      

## Grouped bar graphs

Grouped bar graphs compare several groups across the same categories. Each category gets a cluster of bars, one for each group. The x-axis labels the categories, and the y-axis shows the measured value. Colors or patterns tell the groups apart, and a legend usually sits on the side or top of the graph.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](amounts_files/figure-html/part_grp_bar_graphs-1.png){fig-align='center' width=60% height=60%}
:::
:::


## Summary bar graphs

Bar graphs that summarize data show a statistic for each category. Each bar shows a value we calculate for one group, such as the mean, median, or total. The x-axis lists the categories, and the y-axis shows the statistic values. These graphs make quick comparisons across categories. Error bars can add the variation or confidence intervals.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](amounts_files/figure-html/part_sum_bar_graphs-1.png){fig-align='center' width=60% height=60%}
:::
:::


## Overlapping bar graphs

Overlapping bar graphs draw several groups on top of each other instead of side by side, so we can compare them on the same scale. However, the graph might be harder to read if the bars are entirely opaque. Making the bars partially transparent helps, and a legend tells the groups apart.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](amounts_files/figure-html/part_overlapping_bar_graphs-1.png){fig-align='center' width=60% height=60%}
:::
:::


## Heatmaps

A heatmap is a graph that shows data as colors. It arranges values in a grid, with categories on the x and y axes, and colors each cell by its value. Heatmaps help show how big something is in two dimensions. A color scale (legend) is often next to the graph to explain what the colors mean.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](amounts_files/figure-html/part_heatmaps-1.png){fig-align='center' width=60% height=60%}
:::
:::


## Waffle charts

Waffle charts are a way to show how a whole thing is divided into parts, just like pie charts. Instead of slices, waffle charts fill a grid with squares or icons, one color for each category. They work best for percentages or proportions of a total. A legend or labels next to each category explain what each color means.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](amounts_files/figure-html/part_waffle_charts-1.png){fig-align='center' width=60% height=60%}
:::
:::


## Cleveland dot plots

A Cleveland dot plot is a graph that helps compare a few different numbers. The graph lists the categories on the side and shows the data with dots along a line. It shows the differences between groups with less ink than a bar graph.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](amounts_files/figure-html/part_cleveland_dot_plots-1.png){fig-align='center' width=60% height=60%}
:::
:::
